Title: Local CI runner equivalent to the forge CI
Created: 2026-10-06
Author: Claude

--- log ---
2026-10-06T03:07Z Claude created

--- body ---
## Context

On 2026-10-05 the forge's hosted runners stopped being provisioned and every PR
blocked with its CI `queued`. The author's direction: first replace the forge's
CI with a local one whose checks are exactly equivalent, then, separately,
reflect on the content of the CI (duplication, missing guarantees). Longer
horizon: a CI native to git for the dyne project; see
`docs/2026-10-05-merge-gate-spec.md` (draft, not part of this ticket).

Measured facts are in `memory/journal/2026/2026-10-06-local-ci-act-equivalence.md`.

## Scope

- `scripts/local-ci.sh`: runs every job of `.github/workflows/CI.yml` with `act`
  under rootless podman, one invocation per job (`act -j` honours only the last
  `-j`), on a fresh clone bind-mounted into the container.
- `ci-local/Containerfile`, `ci-local/containers.conf`: runner image (non-root
  uid 1000, podman, passwordless sudo, pip-writable system Python) and a
  dedicated podman service with a `keep-id` user namespace.
- The workflow file stays the single definition of the checks. No check is
  copied, moved or reworded.
- Not in scope: changing the content of the checks; the merge gate; dyne.

## Exit criteria

- [x] `scripts/local-ci.sh` runs the 10 jobs and exits 0 on a clean tree
      (715c59d7, 2026-10-06).
- [ ] It exits non-zero when the job list is empty or differs from the
      workflow's, when `.actrc` is present, and when any job fails.
      Observed: `.actrc` present exits 2; a failing job exits 1. Not yet
      exercised: an empty or differing job list.
- [x] A negative control per guard (9 jobs) fails at its matching step; the
      driver treats a failure outside a `Main` step as an infrastructure
      failure, never as a catch. Kept as a repository test, not a scratch script
      (`scripts/local-ci-negative-controls.sh`, opt-in test
      `tests/test_local_ci_negative_controls.py` with RUN_LOCAL_CI_NEGATIVE=1).
- [x] `cross-pr-ticket-collision` has a positive control for BOTH paths: the
      base-ref collision, and the sibling-PR collision through `gh`, which
      fired on 2026-10-06 ("ticket ID 1041 is also added by open PR #1218")
      when the runner did not recognise its own PR. The image lacked `gh`
      until this ticket added it. The runner now finds its PR by HEAD sha, not
      branch name, so a PR is not its own sibling.
- [x] The summary of a run states that only committed HEAD is tested.
- [x] Parity with a green forge run on the same commit (715c59d7): same 10
      jobs, 1487 passed / 10 skipped on both sides, `grep` 3.11, `bash` 5.2.21,
      `awk` GNU Awk 5.2.1. Parity on failure too: on PR 1218 the forge and the
      runner failed the same two jobs (agnostic-guard; pytest-guard, same two
      tests) on near-identical commits.
- [x] `ci-local/` is tracked (the catch-all ignore rule re-includes it).
- [x] The forge's full suite passes on 715c59d7; `make lint` passed locally on
      the earlier commit and was not re-run on this one.

## Known limits, to record rather than fix here

- The jobs receive the user's `gh` token (act ignores job permissions).
  Acceptable while the only code that runs is the author's own.
- The forge tests the merge of the PR into its base; this tests the branch head.
- `--bind` skips `actions/checkout`; the image approximates `ubuntu-latest`
  (no `EXTERNALLY-MANAGED` marker, `/usr/local` writable).
- The `cross-pr-ticket-collision` job still reads open PRs from the forge's API.
